---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: 'Zach Gorhum',
  jobTitle: 'Certified Public Accountant',
  worksFor: { '@id': 'https://kanonadvisory.com/#business' },
  url: 'https://kanonadvisory.com/about/',
  address: { '@type': 'PostalAddress', addressLocality: 'Houston', addressRegion: 'TX', addressCountry: 'US' },
};
---
<PageLayout
  title="About | Kanon Tax & Advisory"
  description="Kanon Tax & Advisory is an independent tax and advisory practice in Houston, Texas, run by a licensed CPA with a Big 4 and consulting background."
  h1="About the practice"
  eyebrow="Who you're working with"
  lede="A licensed CPA, working directly with a limited number of clients, on the situations that actually require one."
  crumbs={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { label: 'About' }]}
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="section" style="padding-top:32px;">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I started Kanon Tax &amp; Advisory after a decade in Big 4 and independent consulting, working
          on technical accounting and finance problems at companies considerably larger than the ones I
          serve here. Foreign currency remeasurement, lease and revenue standards, ERP-era ledgers,
          multi-entity consolidations.
        </p>
        <p>
          What that background is actually good for, applied at this scale, is judgment about which
          problems are real. A great deal of small-business and individual tax work is people worrying
          about the wrong things while something genuinely expensive goes unnoticed.
        </p>

        <h2>Why these situations</h2>
        <p>
          The returns that go wrong are rarely the complicated-looking ones. They are the ones where
          something changed and nobody adjusted for it: equity that vested and was withheld at a flat
          supplemental rate, a surviving spouse moving from joint to single brackets, a business that
          picked up a remote employee in another state.
        </p>
        <p>
          These situations have three things in common. They are high-stakes, they happen once so nobody
          has practice at them, and the software-driven approach handles them badly because it is built
          on the assumption that this year resembles last year.
        </p>

        <h2>How the practice is set up</h2>
        <p>
          Kanon Tax &amp; Advisory operates as a DBA of Gorhum Enterprises, LLC. I hold an active CPA
          license and am authorized to represent taxpayers before the IRS.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We do not provide attest services.</strong> No audits, reviews, or compilations. If a
          lender or investor requires one, I will say so directly and help you scope it with a firm that
          performs them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>We are not a registered investment adviser.</strong> I advise on the tax consequences of
          decisions. I do not recommend securities, manage assets, or tell you whether to hold or sell a
          position. Where you have a financial adviser, I would rather coordinate with them than work
          around them.
        </p>

        <h2>Working together</h2>
        <p>
          Clients are across the country; nothing here requires you to be in Houston. Documents move
          through an encrypted portal rather than email. Most conversations happen by video or phone, and
          the first one is free and usually tells us both whether this is a fit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CtaBand
    heading="Have a situation worth a conversation?"
    body="If you're not sure whether it's complicated enough to need a CPA, that question is itself a good reason to ask."
  />
</PageLayout>
